  it's the thing most likely to make you unhappy later.

  [1mA time-based notification needs something running at the time it fires.[0m A purely local browser app isn't running when
  the tab is closed. So "remind me this is due at 3pm" can't actually reach you at 3pm unless the page is open at 3pm.

  - [1mIn-page catch-up only[0m — the page computes what's due/overdue/stale whenever you open it and shows it. Always
    correct, needs no permissions, works offline. But it never reaches you; you have to come to it. Effectively a "what
    needs attention" panel.
  - [1mBrowser Notification API[0m — real OS notifications, so they can pull you back while the tab is backgrounded. Needs a
    permission prompt, and still only fires while the tab is open. Small addition on top of the above.
  - [1mService worker + push[0m — the only option that reaches you with the app fully closed, and it requires a server plus
    push infrastructure. That contradicts the local-only decision we just made.

  [1mMy recommendation: the first, with the second as a small opt-in on top.[0m The catch-up panel is the honest core — it's
  always right and never lies about coverage. Browser notifications are maybe twenty lines more and help during a
  working session. Real push is a different project with a server in it, and I'd rather you choose that deliberately
  than have it smuggled in.

  Worth saying plainly: if what you actually pictured was "my phone buzzes when a task is due," none of the local
  options deliver that, and we should back up to the substrate question rather than build something that quietly doesn't
  do what you wanted.
